# `__modname__` command group

`weld add module __modname__ --command` generated this package. weld writes it
once and records every file in `weld.json`; it does not regenerate them, so your
edits survive later `weld add` commands.

## What it is

A command group backed by the `internal/modules/__modname__` business module: it
receives the same `Service` interface and `NewService` constructor the HTTP
handler uses, so the command and `GET /api/__modname__` share one business layer.
A bare `__name__ __modname__` shows help; the generated `greet` subcommand is a
focused example of the seam that you can extend or replace.

## How it is wired

Generating the module command installed `http` and `loom` (and the `config` they
require). The group resolves the module's Service from the command-specific
graph in `internal/di/__modname___graph.go`. That graph binds
`__modname__.NewService` (the same factory the HTTP server graph binds) and
includes `commonModule`, the shared provider set `di.go` declares (configuration,
logger and the installed db/redis/mail/storage bindings); Loom prunes every
provider a subcommand does not consume, so the group never constructs the HTTP
server, the scheduler or a database pool. A capability installed after this
command still reaches it, because the shared providers live in `commonModule`
rather than in this stable graph.

`internal/app/__modname___command.go` registers the group through
`app.RegisterCommand` and delegates `Build` to `di.Init__ModName__`. The `greet`
subcommand calls `g.resolve`, which builds and starts the graph for that one
invocation and stops it within a bounded deadline. Keep the module's `Service`
interface and `NewService` symbol stable; `internal/di/loom_gen.go` is generated
by Loom and must never be edited.
